    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8cd7efe7_ec4bece7",
        "filename": "src/opts/SkSwizzler_opts.inc",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 124,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-24T15:17:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not sure how this was working before without a cast to uint32_t, but `vrndnq_f32` would have the same problem since they round to an integral floating point value, not an actual bitwise integer that `pixel_round_as_RP` is meant to return.\n\nI\u0027m also not sure about wholesale disabling the use of a scalar function when it\u0027s supposedly available for an architecture that we are not otherwise supporting or testing. What is the context for `vrndnq_f32` being more supported than `vrndns_f32`?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 123,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 124,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "165450220ef432c4b40bba918b124b995f7ff0b3",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8c79fc76_a2049b5c",
        "filename": "src/opts/SkSwizzler_opts.inc",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 124,
      "author": {
        "id": 5970
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-24T18:48:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e What is the context for vrndnq_f32 being more supported than vrndns_f32?\n\nThe QNX compiler for the platform I was targeting seems to be based on GCC 8.3. See https://godbolt.org/z/oK9z9j9Ea for a reproduction of the issue on that compiler. I could just switch to scalar version on __QNX__ but this seemed to be more portable. Another option is for me to check if the compiler is GCC (with some version checks) and do this while keeping the scalar verion be.\n\nTo be honest, I didn\u0027t get why SIMD was used here or why the different archs have not quite the same semantics. In tha armv7 version, I don\u0027t think 0.5 should be added either.",
      "parentUuid": "8cd7efe7_ec4bece7",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 123,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 124,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "165450220ef432c4b40bba918b124b995f7ff0b3",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "144b2ab2_9adf7be0",
        "filename": "src/opts/SkSwizzler_opts.inc",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 124,
      "author": {
        "id": 5970
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-24T18:59:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Implemented in https://gcc.gnu.org/git/gitweb.cgi?p\u003dgcc.git;h\u003d02b5377b3766804059b7824330d33d0e1cef2e5b",
      "parentUuid": "8c79fc76_a2049b5c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 123,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 124,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "165450220ef432c4b40bba918b124b995f7ff0b3",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "57a4ab2b_be921808",
        "filename": "src/opts/SkSwizzler_opts.inc",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 124,
      "author": {
        "id": 5140
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-27T17:08:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "float32x4_t vrndnq_f32(float32x4_t a): Floating-point Round to Integral, to nearest with ties to even (vector). \n\nIt looks like the only difference here is that while `vrndns_f32` is an intrisic documented by ARM, there is no instruction for it.\n\nI do need to point out that the complaint here is that `vrndns_f32` isn\u0027t available in GCC 8.3.0, however it is in GCC 8.5.0 (GCC commmit 852423cd68b403d09a14f6436080243c609a57a8). It seems rather unfotunate to make this kind of change when a minor version compiler update fixed this issue (and presumably many more). That being said, I suppose we\u0027ve been trying to support Firefox which currently claims to support GCC 8.1, it seems strange that this issue hasn\u0027t come up there.\n\nNote that GCC 8 was the first to support C++17 and we\u0027re currently trying to drop support for GCC 8 so we can move to C++20 (which means at least GCC 10, though maybe 11).",
      "parentUuid": "144b2ab2_9adf7be0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 123,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 124,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "165450220ef432c4b40bba918b124b995f7ff0b3",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1d44cd62_cc2f4d48",
        "filename": "src/opts/SkSwizzler_opts.inc",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 124,
      "author": {
        "id": 5970
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-01-27T18:58:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e It seems rather unfotunate to make this kind of change when a minor version compiler update fixed this issue (and presumably many more). \n\nThat\u0027s entirely fair. And the very next QNX SDK does bump the version. I can also just sidestep this issues using a GCC specific check like `(__clang__ || (__GNUC__ \u003e\u003d 3 \u0026\u0026 __GNUC_MINOR__ \u003e 2))` and fallback to the scalar backup which does exist.\n\n\u003e That being said, I suppose we\u0027ve been trying to support Firefox which currently claims to support GCC 8.1, it seems strange that this issue hasn\u0027t come up there.\n\nPerhaps Firefox doesn\u0027t build for arm64 using that toolchain? Not sure.\n\nThat said, I am ambivalent about 1) using `vrndnq_f32`, 2) using a GCC specific guard, or 3) adding a `__QNX__` guard. The next QNX version will fix this and more anyway. 4) Any other suggestion that would avoid maintaining Skia patches OOB to build on QNX. Any guidance would be appreciated.",
      "parentUuid": "57a4ab2b_be921808",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 123,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 124,
        "endChar": 29
      },
      "revId": "165450220ef432c4b40bba918b124b995f7ff0b3",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
